{{nihongo|'''Korekiyo Takahashi'''|高橋 是清|Takahashi Korekiyo}} was a Sanese politician and financial expert who served as the 11th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1922 to 1923. A [[Rikken Seiyūkai]] leader who took over as both prime minister and party president after the assassination of [[Kenzan Mihashi]], Takahashi's government struggled to manage the domestic political chaos and the deepening economic Depression, leading to his resignation after only sixteen months in office. He is better remembered for his later service as Minister of Finance, where his pioneering fiscal policies pulled Okaiken out of the Depression.

== Early life ==
Korekiyo Takahashi was born on 10 July 1854 in [[Kofu Prefecture|Kofu]], the illegitimate son of a court painter and a merchant's daughter. He was adopted by the Takahashi family, a samurai household of modest means, and raised in the traditions of the warrior class. His unusual origins—born outside the samurai caste but raised within it—gave him a unique perspective on the social hierarchies of the Saito state.

Takahashi attended the prefectural school in Kofu before winning a scholarship to [[Okami Imperial University]], where he studied economics. He was a brilliant student, particularly gifted in mathematics and monetary theory. After graduating in 1877, he was recruited by the Ministry of Finance, where he would spend the next three decades.

Takahashi's early career in the Ministry of Finance was distinguished by his expertise in international finance and monetary policy. He served as a financial attaché at the Sanese embassy in [[Moselle]] from 1885 to 1890, where he negotiated several key loans that financed the expansion of the Sanese railway network. His fluency in Mosellan and his understanding of [[Volisania|Volisanian]] financial markets made him one of the most effective financial diplomats of his generation.

== Political career ==
After retiring from the Ministry of Finance in 1905, Takahashi entered politics as a member of the [[Rikken Seiyūkai]]. His decision to enter party politics was controversial: many of his former colleagues in the bureaucracy viewed it as a betrayal of the non-partisan tradition of the civil service. But Takahashi believed that the party system was the only vehicle through which his economic ideas could be implemented.

As a Seiyūkai member, Takahashi became the party's leading voice on economic policy. He advocated for deficit spending to stimulate the economy—a radical position in an era when fiscal conservatism was the norm. His economic ideas were ahead of their time and would not be widely adopted until the post-war period.

When [[Kenzan Mihashi]] was assassinated on 1 August 1922, Takahashi was the natural choice to succeed him as both prime minister and president of the Rikken Seiyūkai. As the party's most respected economic thinker and a former finance minister, he was seen as the figure best equipped to continue Mihashi's reform program while managing the economic crisis.

== Premiership (1922–1923) ==
Takahashi was appointed Prime Minister on 1 August 1922, the same day as Mihashi's assassination. His government inherited a nation in crisis: the political chaos following Mihashi's murder, a deepening economic depression, and a military establishment that was increasingly hostile to civilian control.

=== Economic Depression ===
[[File:Takahashi Depression Economic Policy 1923.png|thumb|right|200px|Takahashi's economic measures anticipated the Keynesian revolution in fiscal policy]]
The Takahashi government's primary challenge was the deepening economic Depression. The nation was experiencing a severe recession, with falling export revenues, rising unemployment, and a growing trade deficit. The Depression had been exacerbated by the global economic downturn and by the military's insistence on maintaining high levels of defense spending.

Takahashi implemented a series of measures that anticipated the Keynesian revolution: he increased government spending on public works, lowered interest rates, and devalued the Sanese yen to boost exports. These policies were initially effective, and the economy began to show signs of recovery in the first half of 1923.

However, the military's budget demands undermined Takahashi's fiscal strategy. The Army and Navy insisted on maintaining their spending levels, consuming resources that Takahashi wanted to redirect toward economic stimulus. The conflict between the prime minister's economic priorities and the military's budget demands paralyzed fiscal policy.

=== Political chaos ===
The political situation following Mihashi's assassination was equally challenging. The Rikken Seiyūkai was divided between Mihashi's reformist followers, who wanted to continue the democratic program, and a conservative faction that sought accommodation with the military. Takahashi, who was primarily an economic expert rather than a political operator, struggled to manage the factional dynamics within his own party.

The opposition parties, sensing weakness, attacked the government's economic record and demanded new elections. The [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]] blocked several of Takahashi's budget proposals, demanding increases in military spending that the prime minister could not accommodate without abandoning his fiscal strategy.

The political crisis came to a head in November 1923, when the Diet rejected the government's budget bill. Takahashi, who had been unable to secure the support of either the military or the opposition parties, concluded that he could not continue in office.

Takahashi resigned on 21 November 1923, after sixteen months in office. He was succeeded by [[Takanobu Kinoshita]], a Navy officer who was appointed to restore stability.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Takahashi government's foreign policy was largely overshadowed by the domestic economic crisis, but several significant developments occurred during his brief tenure. The government continued the Mosellan-Sanese trade relationship established under Mihashi, and Takahashi—drawing on his extensive Mosellan contacts from his days as a financial attaché—negotiated a supplementary trade agreement that reduced tariffs on Sanese textile exports to Mosellan markets. The agreement provided a modest boost to the struggling export sector, though it was insufficient to offset the broader effects of the Depression.

In the Shendan, the Takahashi government maintained the commercial diplomacy approach of its predecessors, but the economic crisis limited the resources available for Shendan commercial expansion. The Shendan Commercial Commission's budget was reduced as part of the government's austerity measures, and several planned trade missions were cancelled. The Illypnian colonial powers, particularly Volisania, took advantage of Okaiken's economic weakness to impose additional trade restrictions on Sanese vessels in the Shendan—a development that the military cited as evidence of the need for a more assertive foreign policy.

The government also faced growing pressure from the Army over Kwangju policy. The Army demanded the expansion of the military advisory mission and the authorization of economic concessions that would have effectively placed Kwangju under Sanese protectorate status. Takahashi resisted these demands, arguing that the nation's economic crisis made expansion inadvisable, but his refusal further inflamed the military's hostility toward his government.

== Later career and death ==
After his resignation, Takahashi remained a member of the Diet and continued to advocate for his economic policies. He served as [[Minister of Finance (Okaiken)|Minister of Finance]] in subsequent governments, where he implemented the "Takahashi Economic Policy"—a program of deficit spending, currency devaluation, and public works that successfully pulled Okaiken out of the recession of the early 1930s.

Takahashi was assassinated on 26 February 1936 during the February 26 Incident, 
[[File:Takahashi February 26 Incident 1936.png|thumb|right|200px|Takahashi was assassinated during the February 26 Incident of 1936]]
a military uprising by young Army officers who opposed his economic policies and his attempts to curb military spending. His death marked the end of the era of civilian economic management and the triumph of the military faction.

== Legacy ==
Korekiyo Takahashi is remembered as one of the most brilliant economic minds in Sanese history. His "Takahashi Economic Policy," implemented during his later tenure as finance minister, is widely regarded as a pioneering example of Keynesian fiscal policy—predating the similar policies adopted in [[Volisania]] and [[Moselle]] by several years.

His brief premiership, however, is remembered less for its economic achievements than for the political chaos and economic Depression that defined it. The government's inability to manage the twin crises of political instability and economic contraction demonstrated the structural weaknesses of the party system under the [[Saito Constitution]]: even a prime minister with a clear economic strategy could not govern effectively when the military and the opposition parties were determined to obstruct.

Takahashi's assassination in 1936 removed the most capable advocate for civilian economic management at a critical juncture, and his death is often cited as one of the turning points that led to the full militarization of the Sanese state.

